  "presentation_summary": [
    "68-year-old African-American man was admitted with 2 days of worsening constant noncolicky right upper quadrant abdominal pain rated 6-8/10.",
    "Pain was accompanied by nausea and vomiting with attempted food intake; he had no fevers or chills.",
    "Nine weeks earlier he had undergone craniotomy for brain abscess drainage and had been receiving high-dose ceftriaxone, caspofungin, metronidazole, and corticosteroids.",
    "Physical exam showed marked right upper quadrant tenderness and positive Murphy sign.",
    "Labs showed normal WBC 5400/mm3, hemoglobin 10.3 g/dL, glucose 157 mg/dL, total bilirubin 0.2 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase 177 IU/L, AST 21 IU/L, and ALT 14 IU/L.",
    "CT showed gallbladder wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, and multiple gallstones."
  ],
